---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { languagePaths, type LanguageProps } from "../../../i18n/routes";

export const getStaticPaths = languagePaths;
const { lang } = Astro.props as LanguageProps;
---
<Base
  title="Your next power user can’t see the screen — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="Accessibility lets people with disabilities use an app that was designed for a mouse and a pair of eyes. MCP lets an agent use it for a power user who wants a thousand photos done at once. Both ask the app the same questions."
  active="blogs"
  lang={lang}
  path="/blogs/your-next-power-user-cant-see-the-screen.html"
  translated={false}
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-17">October 17, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Your next power user can&rsquo;t see the screen</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          Accessibility lets people with disabilities use an app that was
          designed for a mouse and a pair of eyes. MCP lets an agent use it
          for a power user who wants a thousand photos done at once. Both
          ask the app the same questions.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 6 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          User experience design is for the typical user: a person with a
          mouse, a screen and some time. Accessibility is for the people that
          design leaves out because of a disability. The agentic experience
          is for the people at the other end, who want an agent to do the
          work of a hundred clicks. A screen reader and an agent need the same
          things from an app: a list of what it can do, a way to do each
          thing by name, and a description of what is on screen.
          LightPhotos draws its interface with egui, which gives a screen
          reader little to read, so reading the app mostly means capturing
          the screen. The fix for agents is one list of 30 commands, plus
          the app's state as text. The keyboard, the menu bar, the control
          channel and the MCP server all use it.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>Two users the design leaves out</h2>
      <p>
        When I design a screen in LightPhotos, I picture one person. They
        sit in front of a laptop, they move a mouse, and they look at the
        photo. Almost every app is designed for that person, and that is
        correct. Most users are that person.
      </p>
      <p>
        Accessibility is the work for the people who are not. A blind
        photographer cannot see the grid, so a screen reader must read it
        to them. A person with a tremor cannot drag a slider to an exact
        value, so the keyboard must do it. Accessibility removes the
        requirement for a particular body. The app still does the same
        things, and more people can tell it to.
      </p>
      <p>
        The agentic experience is for a different person that the design
        also leaves out: the power user. After a wedding, I have 1,200
        photos. I want to say &ldquo;reject every photo where the eyes are
        closed, rate the sharpest of each burst three stars, and give the
        rest the warm preset,&rdquo; and then go and eat dinner. No screen
        layout makes that fast, because the limit is my hand. One person
        cannot click 1,200 times without mistakes. An agent can.
      </p>
      <p>
        So accessibility lets the app do everything for people who cannot
        use the default input. MCP lets the app do more than one person
        could do with that input. These two goals look opposite, but they
        need the same changes to the app. A blind photographer and an agent
        have one thing in common. Neither of them can see the screen.
      </p>

      <h2>The same three questions</h2>
      <p>
        A screen reader cannot see pixels. It asks the operating system
        about the app, through the accessibility tree, and it asks three
        questions:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>What is on the screen? Each control has a name, a role and a value.</li>
        <li>What can I do here? Each control lists its actions, such as press or increment.</li>
        <li>What happened? The app announces a change, so the user knows the action worked.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        LightPhotos answers these questions badly. Its interface is drawn
        with <a href="https://github.com/emilk/egui">egui</a>, which paints
        every button, slider and thumbnail into one GPU frame, 60 times a
        second. The operating system sees a window full of pixels. egui can
        describe some widgets through AccessKit, but a photo grid and a
        develop panel give it very little to say. For a screen reader,
        most of LightPhotos is one large image. The only way to read it is
        to capture the screen and guess what the pixels mean.
      </p>
      <p>
        An agent has the same problem. It can take a screenshot and look at
        it, and the MCP server has a <code>screenshot</code> tool for that.
        But a screenshot is a slow, lossy way to learn that photo 412 has
        three stars. An agent that uses MCP asks the same three questions
        as a screen reader, and it needs the answers as text. In
        LightPhotos, they are four tools. <code>list_commands</code> answers &ldquo;what can I
        do?&rdquo; <code>get_state</code> and <code>list_photos</code> answer
        &ldquo;what is on the screen?&rdquo; <code>run_command</code> does
        one thing, and its reply says what happened, or why it failed.
      </p>
      <p>
        An app that answers these questions well for a screen reader is
        most of the way to an app that answers them for an agent. An app
        that answers them for neither is a set of pixels that only one kind
        of person can use.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="reader-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls" role="group" aria-label="Who reads the app">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-reader="sr" aria-pressed="true">Screen reader</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-reader="shot" aria-pressed="false">Agent with a screenshot</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-reader="mcp" aria-pressed="false">Agent with MCP</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 236" role="img" aria-label="Interactive: the same LightPhotos window, and what a screen reader, an agent with a screenshot, or an agent with MCP gets from it.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">THE SAME EGUI WINDOW</text>
